// pdfcer-gui — the Windows resource script.
//
// Compiled by `build.rs` through the `embed-resource` crate, which finds
// `rc.exe` from the installed Windows SDK (or `windres` on a cross build) and
// links the result into the executable's PE image.
//
// ===========================================================================
// WHY AN ICON HAS TO BE A RESOURCE AND CANNOT BE A RUNTIME ASSET
// ===========================================================================
//
// The operator's request, 2026-08-18: *"make and add a pdf icon to the exe so
// it shows as the icon when I associate it with pdfs."*
//
// Everything that shows that icon — Explorer, the Start menu, the taskbar's
// pinned entry, and the *Open with* / file-association dialog — reads it from
// the executable **without running it**. So it cannot be loaded at start-up,
// cannot be a file beside the binary, and cannot be drawn by the application:
// it has to be inside the PE image, in the `.rsrc` section, where the shell
// can find it with `ExtractIcon`.
//
// That is also why the portable distribution needs no extra file for this. The
// icon travels inside `pdfcer-gui.exe`, which is the whole point of shipping
// one self-contained executable.
//
// ===========================================================================
// WHY THE ICON IS RESOURCE ID 1
// ===========================================================================
//
// The shell shows the icon with the **lowest numeric ID** as an executable's
// representative icon. It is a convention rather than a documented rule, and
// it is universally relied on; a second icon added later must take a higher
// number or it will silently become the application's face.

1 ICON "pdfcer-gui.ico"

// ===========================================================================
// VERSIONINFO — the properties tab, and why it is worth filling in
// ===========================================================================
//
// Without this block the executable's Properties ▸ Details tab is blank: no
// name, no version, no company. That matters more than it sounds for a program
// an operator associates with a file type, because it is what Windows shows in
// the *Open with* list, in SmartScreen prompts, and in Task Manager's
// description column. A blank one reads as an unidentified program.
//
// The two version numbers are the same fact twice: `FILEVERSION` is a
// four-integer field Windows sorts on, and `FileVersion` is the string a human
// reads. They are kept in step by hand here — the crate version lives in
// `Cargo.toml`, and wiring it through would mean generating this file, which is
// a build step for a number that changes once a release.

// ===========================================================================
// ★ EVERY STRING BELOW IS ASCII, AND THAT IS NOT AN AESTHETIC CHOICE
// ===========================================================================
//
// `rc.exe` reads a `.rc` file as the system ANSI code page unless it is given
// `/c65001` or the file carries a UTF-8 BOM. This file is UTF-8 (the comments
// above contain em dashes and a star), and the first version of the block
// below used an em dash in `FileDescription`. It compiled, linked, and shipped
// — and Windows Properties showed:
//
//     pdfcer â€” PDF viewer and editor
//
// UTF-8 bytes rendered as CP1252, in the one place an operator looks to find
// out what an unfamiliar executable is. Nothing failed and nothing warned.
//
// The fix could have been a BOM or a compiler flag routed through
// `embed-resource`; it is ASCII instead, because a version block has no need
// of typography and an encoding dependency that only shows up in a properties
// dialog is one nobody will remember to re-check.

1 VERSIONINFO
FILEVERSION    0,1,0,0
PRODUCTVERSION 0,1,0,0
FILEOS         0x4L   // VOS_NT_WINDOWS32
FILETYPE       0x1L   // VFT_APP
{
    BLOCK "StringFileInfo"
    {
        // 0409 = US English, 04B0 = Unicode. The pair is the langID+charsetID
        // key Windows looks the strings up under; a mismatched pair makes the
        // whole block invisible rather than erroring.
        BLOCK "040904B0"
        {
            VALUE "CompanyName",      "Ken Mantle"
            VALUE "FileDescription",  "pdfcer - PDF viewer and editor"
            VALUE "FileVersion",      "0.1.0"
            VALUE "InternalName",     "pdfcer-gui"
            VALUE "LegalCopyright",   "Copyright (c) 2026 Ken Mantle. MIT licensed."
            VALUE "OriginalFilename", "pdfcer-gui.exe"
            VALUE "ProductName",      "pdfcer"
            VALUE "ProductVersion",   "0.1.0"
        }
    }
    BLOCK "VarFileInfo"
    {
        // Must agree with the StringFileInfo block above, as two integers
        // rather than as a string. This is the classic VERSIONINFO mistake and
        // its symptom is a Details tab that is still blank after all of the
        // above was written correctly.
        VALUE "Translation", 0x409, 1200
    }
}
